From 9efca44e84c034d0d29198ddc4250c49ce929622 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 11:37:10 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10899/11171] Record that one daily-timer fix was exercised in
 production and one was not

Both services are green after cf10ba7c61, but they are not equally proven and
the record should say so rather than reporting a uniform success.

The prep timer's 13:37Z run took about 17 minutes and exited 0. The old poll
gave up at 6, so this run genuinely required the new 3600s budget -- the fix is
exercised, not merely present. Kernel v54, save_run complete, parquet_ok true.

The news watch is the opposite. Its verification run took 2m52s, which the old
300s budget would also have passed, so it demonstrates a healthy unit and
nothing about the raised timeout. The band the 900s budget exists to rescue is
4 to 8 minutes, where 7 of the last 12 daily runs were failing. Recorded as
unproven rather than folded into the success.

That gap needs no follow-up task because the check now records its own budget:
the timeout message reads CHECK_TIMED_OUT_900s, so a recurrence names what it
exceeded instead of being anonymous, and a timeout at 900s points at codex or
web latency rather than the script.

